                    <td>2010s</td>
                    <td>7.67</td>
                    <td>117–153</td>
                    <td class="trend-down">-10.0%</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>2020s</td>
                    <td>7.80</td>
                    <td>84–137</td>
                    <td class="trend-up">+1.7%</td>
                </tr>
            </table>
        </section>

        <!-- Section 2: Regional Comparison -->
        <section id="regional-comparison">
            <h2>Regional & Country-Level Comparison</h2>
            <p>Homicide rates vary dramatically across countries and regions. The Americas remain the most affected region globally, while East Asia and Western Europe generally report the lowest rates.</p>

            <h3>Countries with Highest Homicide Rates (2023)</h3>
            <div class="country-list">
                <div class="country-item"><span>Saint Kitts and Nevis</span><strong>64.16</strong></div>
                <div class="country-item"><span>Saint Vincent and the Grenadines</span><strong>51.32</strong></div>
                <div class="country-item"><span>Jamaica</span><strong>49.44</strong></div>
                <div class="country-item"><span>Ecuador</span><strong>45.72</strong></div>
                <div class="country-item"><span>Haiti</span><strong>41.15</strong></div>
                <div class="country-item"><span>Saint Lucia</span><strong>39.04</strong></div>
                <div class="country-item"><span>Honduras</span><strong>31.44</strong></div>
                <div class="country-item"><span>Dominica</span><strong>27.06</strong></div>
                <div class="country-item"><span>Colombia</span><strong>24.91</strong></div>
                <div class="country-item"><span>Mexico</span><strong>24.86</strong></div>
            </div>

            <p>These ten countries—all located in the Caribbean or Latin America—account for some of the world's highest homicide rates. Saint Kitts and Nevis, with a rate of 64.16 per 100,000, experiences homicide levels more than 35 times higher than the global median.</p>

            <div class="chart-container">
                <canvas id="topCountriesChart"></canvas>
            </div>

            <h3>Geographic Concentration of High Homicide Rates</h3>
            <p>Of the 15 countries with homicide rates exceeding 20 per 100,000 in 2023, all are located in:</p>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Caribbean</strong>: Saint Kitts and Nevis, Saint Vincent and the Grenadines, Jamaica, Saint Lucia, Dominica, Grenada, Trinidad and Tobago</li>
                <li><strong>Central America</strong>: Honduras, Guatemala, El Salvador, Belize</li>
                <li><strong>South America</strong>: Colombia, Venezuela, Brazil</li>
                <li><strong>Other</strong>: Ecuador (Andean region)</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <!-- Section 3: Gender Disparity -->
        <section id="sex-disparity">
            <h2>Gender Disparity in Homicide Victimization</h2>
            <p>One of the most consistent and stark findings across all countries and time periods is the significant gender disparity in homicide victimization. Men are far more likely to be homicide victims than women, though both sexes remain affected.</p>

            <div class="kpi-dashboard">
                <div class="kpi-card danger">
                    <div class="kpi-label">Global Male-to-Female Ratio</div>
                    <div class="kpi-value">6.85x</div>
                    <div class="kpi-context">Men are nearly 7x more likely to be homicide victims</div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="chart-container">
                <canvas id="genderTrendChart"></canvas>
            </div>

            <p>The male-to-female homicide ratio has remained persistently high throughout the observation period, ranging from a low of 4.22 (1994) to a high of 6.91 (2016). In 2023, the ratio stands at 6.85, meaning men are nearly seven times more likely to be murdered than women globally.</p>

            <h3>Countries with Highest Male-to-Female Ratios (2023)</h3>
            <p>These ratios indicate where male homicide rates dramatically exceed female rates:</p>
            <table>
                <tr>
                    <th>Country</th>
                    <th>Male Rate</th>
                    <th>Female Rate</th>
                    <th>Ratio</th>
                </tr>
                <tr><td>Barbados</td><td>14.77</td><td>0.68</td><td>21.7x</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Saint Kitts and Nevis</td><td>124.96</td><td>8.21</td><td>15.2x</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Grenada</td><td>25.53</td><td>1.71</td><td>14.9x</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Colombia</td><td>46.95</td><td>3.44</td><td>13.6x</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Panama</td><td>21.71</td><td>1.70</td><td>12.7x</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Ecuador</td><td>84.51</td><td>6.66</td><td>12.7x</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Costa Rica</td><td>33.09</td><td>2.75</td><td>12.0x</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Puerto Rico</td><td>27.69</td><td>2.39</td><td>11.6x</td></tr>
            </table>

            <div class="highlight">
                <strong>Policy Implication:</strong> While homicide affects both sexes, prevention strategies must account for the distinct risk profiles of men and women. Male homicide victims are predominantly young adults (15-44), often linked to gang activity, organized crime, or interpersonal violence. Female homicide victims frequently experience intimate partner violence as a contributing factor.
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- Section 4: Age Group Analysis -->
        <section id="age-group-analysis">
            <h2>Age Group Analysis</h2>
            <p>Analysis of homicide rates by age group reveals that young adults face the highest risk of homicide victimization across most reporting countries.</p>

            <div class="chart-container small">
                <canvas id="ageGroupChart"></canvas>
            </div>

            <p>Based on the most recent age-disaggregated data available (2022), the patterns are clear:</p>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Young adults (20-29)</strong> face the highest homicide risk, with rates peaking in the late teens and twenties</li>
                <li><strong>Middle-aged adults (30-44)</strong> experience elevated but declining rates</li>
                <li><strong>Adolescents (15-17)</strong> show moderate but notable rates</li>
                <li><strong>Children (0-14)</strong> and <strong>elderly (60+)</strong> have the lowest rates</li>
            </ul>

            <h3>Country Variations in Age Patterns</h3>
            <p>The age profile of homicide victims varies by country. In countries with high overall homicide rates (e.g., in Central America and the Caribbean), the concentration in young adult males is particularly pronounced. In contrast, countries with lower overall rates tend to show a more evenly distributed age pattern or slightly higher elderly victimization.</p>
        </section>

        <!-- Section 5: Notable Trends & Outliers -->
        <section id="notable-trends">
            <h2>Notable Country-Level Trends & Outliers</h2>
            <p>Beyond static comparisons, examining longitudinal changes reveals dramatically different trajectories across countries.</p>

            <h3>Countries with Greatest Increases (1990/earliest → 2023)</h3>
            <div class="chart-container small">
                <canvas id="increaseChart"></canvas>
            </div>

            <table>
                <tr>
                    <th>Country</th>
                    <th>Period</th>
                    <th>Rate Change</th>
                    <th>% Change</th>
                </tr>
                <tr><td>Haiti</td><td>2007→2023</td><td>5.18 → 41.15</td><td class="trend-up">+694%</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Saint Kitts and Nevis</td><td>1995→2023</td><td>9.45 → 64.16</td><td class="trend-up">+579%</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Antigua and Barbuda</td><td>1990→2023</td><td>1.59 → 10.72</td><td class="trend-up">+576%</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Ecuador</td><td>1990→2023</td><td>8.52 → 45.72</td><td class="trend-up">+437%</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Saint Vincent and the Grenadines</td><td>1990→2023</td><td>9.84 → 51.32</td><td class="trend-up">+422%</td></tr>
            </table>

            <h3>Countries with Greatest Decreases (1990/earliest → 2022/2023)</h3>
            <div class="chart-container small">
                <canvas id="decreaseChart"></canvas>
            </div>

            <table>
                <tr>
                    <th>Country</th>
                    <th>Period</th>
                    <th>Rate Change</th>
                    <th>% Change</th>
                </tr>
                <tr><td>El Salvador</td><td>1994→2022</td><td>134.28 → 7.90</td><td class="trend-down">-94%</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Singapore</td><td>1990→2023</td><td>1.55 → 0.07</td><td class="trend-down">-96%</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Qatar</td><td>1990→2022</td><td>0.91 → 0.07</td><td class="trend-down">-92%</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Turkmenistan</td><td>1990→2015</td><td>8.83 → 1.01</td><td class="trend-down">-89%</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Oman</td><td>2002→2023</td><td>1.20 → 0.14</td><td class="trend-down">-88%</td></tr>
            </table>

            <div class="highlight">
                <strong>Notable Success Story:</strong> El Salvador's decline from 134.28 (1994) to 7.90 (2022) represents one of the most dramatic homicide reductions ever documented. While controversial security policies contributed to this decline, the case demonstrates that even the most homicide-plagued societies can achieve significant improvements.
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- Section 6: Data Limitations & Recommendations -->
        <section id="data-limitations-recommendations">
            <h2>Data Limitations & Policy Recommendations</h2>

            <div class="warning-box">
                <h3>Critical Data Limitations</h3>
                <p>This analysis is based on an <strong>unbalanced panel dataset</strong> with significant reporting gaps. Key limitations include:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Incomplete time series:</strong> Not all countries have observations for every year. Coverage ranges from 84 countries (2023) to 162 countries (2008).</li>
